  "rules": "Independence key = publisher_group. Two items are NOT independent if (a) their domains map to the same group in domain_to_group, (b) either one is wire copy of the same agency (detect via byline markers below) - then both collapse to the WIRE's group (e.g. two outlets running the same PTI story count as ONE: 'pti'), (c) one is an aggregator page (Yahoo/MSN/Dailyhunt/Google News) - attribute it to the underlying provider when named, else drop it from independence counting, (d) titles are near-identical (e.g. normalized token Jaccard >= 0.8) and published within ~48h - treat as syndicated even if no marker is visible. Wire-copy detection, strongest first: (1) feed fields: Business Standard <bs:source>/<dc:creator> = 'Press Trust of India'; Republic <author> '(Asian News International)'; (2) page disclaimer 'the rest of the content is auto-generated from a syndicated feed' (Business Standard, observed) or 'has not been edited by <X> staff' style notes; (3) a byline/dateline token 'PTI' / 'ANI' / 'IANS' / 'UNI' / 'Reuters' / 'AFP' / 'AP' immediately after the city+date or as the author (e.g. HT: 'Published on: Oct 31, 2026 ... IST PTI'; The Hindu: 'New Delhi PTI'); (4) a parenthesized dateline '(PTI)' / '(ANI)' / '(IANS)' at the start of the body ('Hyderabad, Oct 30 (ANI):'); (5) closing note 'With inputs from PTI/ANI/agencies' => PARTIAL syndication: the outlet added its own reporting; count it as its own group but flag 'wire_derived'. CAUTION (observed 2026-10-31): bare 'PTI'/'ANI' tokens appear mostly in PHOTO CREDITS ('(PTI Photo)', 'Photo Credit: ANI', 'File Photo: ANI', image alt text) and in attributions ('told PTI', 'news agency ANI reported'). Those are NOT syndication - only use tokens in byline/dateline/author positions. Same-group examples: a Vishvas News fact check + a Dainik Jagran story = one group (jagran); Aaj Tak + India Today + The Lallantop = one group; Indian Express and New Indian Express are DIFFERENT owners. Government outlets (PIB, DD, AIR, ministries) = one group 'govt_india'."
